[
    {
        "content": "<p>Hello everyone,</p>\n<p>my group (<span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"679752\">@Viviana del Barco</span>, <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"926237\">@Gustavo Infanti</span>, <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"779909\">@Exequiel Rivas</span>) has recently become interested in doing work on algebras with are not necessarily associative or unital. More specifically: we want to formalize a classification of arbitrary algebras, and in particular Leibniz algebras, in low dimensions, in the same spirit as what we did for Lie algebras (<a class=\"stream-topic\" data-stream-id=\"113486\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/113486-General-announcements/topic/Classification.20results.20for.20Lie.20algebras/with/520790476\">#General announcements &gt; Classification results for Lie algebras</a> )</p>\n<p>From my crawling of the Zulip and the mathlib docs I found that the current way of treating these general algebras is not by using a separate typeclass, but by e.g.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Semiring</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">NonUnitalNonAssocRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Module</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">SMulCommClass</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsScalarTower</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>cf. <a class=\"message-link\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/116395-maths/topic/On.20undergraduate.20topics.20missing.20in.20mathlib/near/577953829\">#maths &gt; On undergraduate topics missing in mathlib @ 💬</a>.</p>\n<p>This is all cool and good, but now we're wondering what the best approach is to implement various machinery for these algebras, foremost the type of (two-sided) ideals of these algebras. Mathlib already has <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/RingTheory/TwoSidedIdeal/Basic.html#TwoSidedIdeal\"><code>TwoSidedIdeal</code></a> which works in any non-unital, non-associative ring and gives the right notion of ideal in <em>unital</em> algebras. However for <em>non-unital</em> ones, being closed under scalar multiplication is an additional requirement that needs to be added. I am therefore proposing a structure <code>NonUnitalNonAssocAlgebra.TwoSidedIdeal</code> that handles this. Right now I see at least three ways to implement this:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Analogous to <code>TwoSidedIdeal</code> being essentially a wrapper for <code>RingCon</code> (I assume to facilitate taking quotients), we make a new <code>AlgebraCon</code> that includes closure under scalar multiplication and write all the API theorems again.</li>\n<li>Reuse as much as possible by extending the structure, i.e.:</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">structure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">NUNAA</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">TwoSidedIdeal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">extends</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">TwoSidedIdeal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">smul'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ringCon</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ringCon</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>just define ideals ad hoc with the traditional definition, i.e.:</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">structure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">NUNAA</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">TwoSidedIdeal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">extends</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">AddSubmonoid</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SubMulAction</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">left_mul_mem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">carrier</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">carrier</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">right_mul_mem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">carrier</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">carrier</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>What do you think would be the best approach here? Or is there even something already in mathlib that we are missing?</p>",
